    level: 1,
    type: "qa",
    classic: true,
    q: "What are undistributed expenses, and why doesn't USALI allocate them to departments?",
    a: "They're the costs of running the hotel as a whole: administrative and general, IT and telecom, sales and marketing, maintenance and utilities. USALI leaves them unallocated because any split would be arbitrary, and each department should be judged only on the revenue and costs its manager controls.",
    why: "USALI is built on responsibility accounting: each department's statement shows only what its manager can influence. Engineering, energy and the sales team serve rooms, restaurants and meeting space at once. Split them by revenue, floor area or headcount and each department's profit changes while the hotel's doesn't. Keeping them in one block makes departmental results comparable across hotels and puts overhead in plain view, where owners can benchmark it. They're also largely fixed, a big source of operating leverage.",
    trap: "Unallocated isn't small: undistributed expenses often run around a quarter of total revenue. And because departmental profit ignores them, a department's margin overstates its full profit, which matters when deciding whether to close a restaurant."
