    topic: "The FF&E reserve line",
    level: 2,
    type: "qa",
    q: "Why is the FF&E reserve deducted below EBITDA rather than treated as an operating expense?",
    a: "Because it isn't an expense. The reserve is cash set aside, commonly a percentage of total revenue, to replace furniture, fixtures and equipment. The purchases it funds are capitalized and depreciated. So USALI shows EBITDA first, then subtracts the reserve to give EBITDA less replacement reserve, a closer measure of recurring cash flow.",
    why: "Hotels wear out fast: carpets, beds, TVs and lobby furniture need replacing every several years, and brands enforce standards. Because that spending is capitalized, EBITDA never reflects it and overstates the cash an owner can take out. The reserve turns lumpy replacement spending into a steady annual charge. Management agreements and loans often require it to be funded, so for many owners it's as real as an expense, which is why buyers and lenders focus on EBITDA less reserve.",
    trap: "In a projection, don't deduct the reserve and also model routine FF&E purchases as capex: that double counts. Big renovations or brand-mandated upgrades beyond what the reserve covers are the exception and are modeled separately."
