    q: "How do you project a hotel's operating expenses in a model?",
    a: "Line by line, split each expense into a fixed part and a variable part. The fixed part is a dollar amount that grows with inflation. The variable part scales with its driver: occupied rooms for rooms costs, F&B revenue for cost of sales, total revenue for fees and card commissions. Undistributed costs are mostly fixed.",
    why: "Projecting every cost as a flat percentage of revenue is the classic modeling mistake. It assumes costs fall in step when revenue falls and rise in step when rate rises, which understates operating leverage in both directions. A fixed and variable split captures it: a rate increase adds revenue with little cost, while an occupancy drop sheds only the variable part. Base the split on the hotel's history and comparable hotels; hotel appraisers commonly use this fixed-and-variable approach.",
    example: "Rooms expense: [[$1.5M]] fixed, growing [[3%]], plus [[$28]] per occupied room. At [[80,000]] occupied rooms next year: $1.545M + $2.24M = $3.785M, or $47.31 per occupied room. At [[70,000]]: $3.505M, or $50.07 per room. Cost per room rises because the fixed part is spread over fewer rooms.",
    trap: "A flat-percentage model gives a 10% ADR increase and a 10% occupancy increase the same profit. A fixed-variable model correctly makes the ADR-led case more profitable, because extra occupancy brings extra variable costs."
