    why: "Labor is a hotel's largest cost, often around a third of revenue at full-service hotels and roughly half of operating expenses. Labor cost per occupied room can rise for two reasons: more hours or higher pay. If hours per occupied room hold steady while cost climbs, it's wage inflation, and the response is productivity projects or pricing. If hours rise, scheduling has slipped: staff aren't being flexed to occupancy. USALI counts contracted and outsourced labor as labor, so outsourcing housekeeping doesn't make the line look better.",
